Q: what&#39;s wrong in this function? (beginner cs50) A: <p>Analyzing this issue and noting the good comment about adding a &quot;break&quot; statement to address the main issue of switching back a character, I did a bit of refactoring to the &quot;replace&quot; function to get the desired outcome for the lowercase &quot;hello&quot; sample.</p>
<pre><code>string replace(string a, string b)
{
    string final = a;
    string alpha = {&quot;abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz&quot;};
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; strlen(a); i++)
    {
        for(int n = 0; n &lt; 26; n++)
        {
            if(a[i] == alpha[n])
            {
                final[i] = b[n];
                break;              /* Per the good comments    */
            }
        }
    }
    return final;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Retesting that example yielded the following terminal output.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encoder/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encoder ytnshkvefxrbauqzclwdmipgjo
plaintext.: hello
ciphertext: ehbbq
</code></pre>
<p>Usually though, when building an encryption or encoding function, it needs to be robust to handle both uppercase and lowercase characters.  At this state, uppercase characters do not get replaced/encoded.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encoder/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encoder ytnshkvefxrbauqzclwdmipgjo
plaintext.: HELLO
ciphertext: HELLO
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encoder/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encoder ytnshkvefxrbauqzclwdmipgjo
plaintext.: Hello
ciphertext: Hhbbq
</code></pre>
<p>So as an embellishment to this issue and solution, following is a refactored version of the function to handle both uppercase and lowercase characters.</p>
<pre><code>string replace(string a, string b)
{
    string final = a;
    string alpha = {&quot;abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz&quot;};
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; strlen(a); i++)
    {
        for(int n = 0; n &lt; 26; n++)
        {
            if (islower(a[i]) &amp;&amp; a[i] == alpha[n])      /* To handle lowercase and uppercase    */
            {
                final[i] = b[n];
                break;
            }
            else if (isupper(a[i]) &amp;&amp; tolower(a[i]) == alpha[n])
            {
                final[i] = toupper(b[n]);
                break;
            }
            else
            {
                continue;
            }
        }
    }
    return final;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Following is the terminal output with various lowercase, uppercase, and mixed case text entries.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encoder/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encoder ytnshkvefxrbauqzclwdmipgjo
plaintext.: hello
ciphertext: ehbbq
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encoder/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encoder ytnshkvefxrbauqzclwdmipgjo
plaintext.: HELLO
ciphertext: EHBBQ
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encoder/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encoder ytnshkvefxrbauqzclwdmipgjo
plaintext.: Hello 
ciphertext: Ehbbq
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/CS50_Encoder/bin/Release$ ./CS50_Encoder ytnshkvefxrbauqzclwdmipgjo
plaintext.: Hello There
ciphertext: Ehbbq Dehlh
</code></pre>
<p>The main takeaway is to probably delve more into some &quot;C&quot; tutorials as it pertains to character manipulation along with the behavior of loops and the &quot;break&quot; statement.</p>

